autopledge
===========

automatically sandbox syscalls available to vulnerable programs

What?
-----

```console
$ LD_PRELOAD=libautopledge.so <program>
```

autopledge statically detects and whitelists the system calls a program needs
at runtime, mitigating the risk of harm from injected shellcode.

autopledge is implemented as a constructor in a preloaded shared library,
allowing it to run after the program is loaded, but before execution begins.

The system calls a program needs to function are detected via the calls the
program makes to dynamically linked libraries, before being restricted with
linux's built-in seccomp(2) facilities.

If the process executes an unexpected system call during runtime (for example
the `execve` of a shell spawn), the linux kernel kills the process to prevent
potentially injected code from harming the system.

#### What autopledge Can Do

 - Restrict arbitrary code execution[^poc]
 - Detect expected system calls in most x86_64 linux binaries
 - Traverse and analyze shared libraries linked to a program
 - Add an extra layer of security to vulnerable, legacy applications

#### What autopledge Cannot Do

 - Prevent shellcode injection
 - Detect all possible system calls a program could make
 - Stop all exploitation and malicious attacks
 - Effectively protect interpreted programs[^interp]

[^poc]: For a good example of what autopledge is good for, check out the
`test/poc-overflow` folder, which contains an example vulnerable program and
shellcode injection exploit which autopledge prevents from succeeding.  In this
case, a buffer overflow results in a vulnerability by which an attacker can
run any program.  By whitelisting the expected system calls of the program,
autopledge prevents the attacker from launching other programs (with `fork` or
`execve`) like a shell.  **Make sure to run this with `make poc-overflow`!  The
process is dependent on the environmental variables and shared library paths
set by the Makefile.**

[^interp]: autopledge is designed with the use case of proprietary and/or fixed version
binaries.  In the case of an interpreted program, the interpreter itself can
be expected to require a vast amount of permissions, any subset of which the
interpreted program may need for itself.  Additionally, interpreted programs
may be modified to eliminate bugs much more readily than programs distributed
as binaries.

Why?
----

In March 2016, OpenBSD 5.9 introduced the pledge(2) system call as a mitigation
feature against arbitrary code execution and privilege abuse.  pledge(2) is a
declaration made by the program at the beginning of execution to only use system
calls from certain categories (e.g., `stdio`, `inet`).  If the program violates
its pledge, it is killed.

Unfortunately, to add support for this to a program, not only must the program
be modifiable (unviable for proprietary or restrictively licensed software), but
the programmer must also know which system calls they need to allow.

The Linux equivalent of pledge(2) is seccomp(2), which allows much finer system
call filtering, is much more difficult to use, and suffers from the same
problem: the program must be modifiable, and the programmer must know which
system calls the program uses.

While the majority of Linux software may be open-source, proprietary software
often presents even more of a security risk (cf. thousand eyes/shallow bugs),
especially legacy software that is past end-of-life and/or internet-facing
software.  Even in open source programs it is often impractical to manually
determine the necessary system calls by hand.

autopledge is designed to make running vulnerable binaries safer by restricting
the abilities of injected shellcode with system call filtering constructed from
static analysis of the program.  autopledge will not prevent shellcode injection
and it is not guaranteed to prevent shellcode from causing harm to a system, but
it presents a first line of defence against exploitation.

How?
----

### Dependencies

autopledge requires the following to build and run successfully[^arch]:

> - **cc** (tested on **gcc** and **clang**)
> - **glibc**
> - **libstdc++**
> - **linux** (>=3.17)

If you plan on utilizing the optional build tools, you will also need:

> - **python** (>=3.10)

The proof-of-concept exploit preventions require:

> - **nasm**

[^arch]: autopledge in its current form will only work on x86_64, because the
arch-specific relocation constants are hardcoded.  This is easily fixed with
some ifdefs, but that's still on the todo list.  Furthermore, autopledge does
not as of yet parse the default library search paths, so any unrecognized
dynamically-linked libaries must be in the working directory at runtime.

### Building

To run the _optional_ build tools and customize the list of glibc and libstdc++
syscalls by function to your system, run:

```console
$ make tools
```

To build autopledge, run:

```console
$ make all
```

#### Advanced Configuration

The build may be customized by providing values on the command line:

```console
$ make <setting>=<value> all
```

By default, autopledge uses syslog for logging (`USE_SYSLOG=1`).  The
alternatives provided are logging to stderr (`USE_STDERR=1`) and disabling
logging (`USE_NOLOGS=1`).

By default, processes that have been started using autopledge are killed if a
non-enabled system call is performed.  A trap signal may be sent instead via
`LENIENT=1`.

The name of the outputted binary (default: `libautopledge.so`) may be set with
`AUTOPLEDGE=<name>`.

### Testing (optional)

To test your build, run:

```console
$ make test
```

The results of the tests will be printed to the screen, but they are also
recorded in `test/test-results`.  An 'S' indicates success, and 'F' indicates
failure.

If a test fails, a log of errors is located in `test/<test name>/<test name>.log`.

#### Advanced Configuration

The file that test results are sent to (default: `test/test-results`) may be
customized with `TESTFILE=<file>`.

Individual tests may be run with:

```console
$ make <test-name>
```

### Installing

To install autopledge on your system, run:

```console
$ sudo make install
```

#### Advanced Configuration

By default, autopledge is installed to `/usr/lib64`.  The prefix
(default: `/usr`) may be set with `PREFIX=<path>`, and the entire destination
folder may be changed by setting `LIBDIR=<path>`.

